  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Lagrange multipliers_Question 3</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Find the maximum and the minimum values of \(f(x,y,z)={@fx@}\) subject to the constraint \(g(x,y,z)={@gx@}\) using the <a href="https://tutorial.math.lamar.edu/Classes/CalcIII/LagrangeMultipliers.aspx">Method of Lagrange Multipliers</a>. By completing the following solution</p>
<p>First, we observe that the constraint region is bounded. It is also closed, therefore it is [[input:ans1s]] [[validation:ans1s]] [[feedback:prt1s]] Now, by the Extreme Value Theorem, we can conclude that the function [[input:ans1ss]][[feedback:prt1ss]]  in the constraint region [[validation:ans1ss]] </p>
<p>The next step is to write down the system of Lagrange Multiplier equations:</p>
<p>[[input:ans1]][[feedback:prt1]] \((f_x=\lambda g_x)...\) equation \(1\) [[validation:ans1]]</p>
<p>[[input:ans2]][[feedback:prt2]] \((f_y=\lambda g_y)...\) equation \(2\) [[validation:ans2]]</p>
<p>[[input:ans3]][[feedback:prt3]] \((f_z=\lambda g_z)...\) equation \(3\) [[validation:ans3]]</p>
<p>[[input:ans4]][[feedback:prt4]] (Constraint equation) \(…\) equation \(4\) [[validation:ans4]]</p>
<p>From the second equation</p>
<p>\(y=\)[[input:ans5]][[feedback:prt5]] (<b>case </b>\(1\)) or \(\lambda=\)[[input:ans6]][[feedback:prt6]] (<b>case </b>\(2\)) [[validation:ans5]] [[validation:ans6]]</p>
<p><b>Case </b>\(1:\)  Plugging in the value of \(y\) into \({@gx@}\) since \(x=0\) 
</p>
<p>\(z=\pm\) [[input:ans7]][[validation:ans7]][[feedback:prt7]]</p>
<p>Hence two potential absolute extrema points are
</p>
<br>[[input:ans8]][[feedback:prt8]] and [[input:ans9]][[validation:ans8]][[validation:ans9]]
<p><b>Case </b>\(2:\) Plugging in the value of \(\lambda\) into equation \(3\)</p>
<p>\(z=\) [[input:ans10]][[validation:ans10]][[feedback:prt10]]</p>
<p>Plugging in the value of \(x\) and \(z\) in \({@gx@}\) </p>
<p>\(y=\pm\) [[input:ans11]][[validation:ans11]][[feedback:prt11]]</p>
<p>This part gives us two more points that are potential absolute extrema,</p>
<br>[[input:ans12]][[feedback:prt12]] and [[input:ans13]][[validation:ans12]][[validation:ans13]]
<p>To find the absolute extrema we evaluate the function at each of these points</p>
<p>The absolute maximum is [[input:ans14]][[validation:ans14]][[feedback:prt14]]</p>
<p>The absolute minimum is [[input:ans15]][[validation:ans15]][[feedback:prt15]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><b>Worked Solution</b></p>
<p>Here the constraint is a sum of two terms that are squared, hence the largest possible range of \(x\)  is \({@x_ranges@}\)  (the largest values occur if \(y=0\)  and \(z=0\)</p>
<p>Likewise,the largest possible range of \(x\)  is \({@x_ranges@}\) (with the largest values occurring if \(x=0\)).
</p>
<p>Therefore, the constraint <b>region is bounded</b>. It is also closed, therefore it is <b>compact</b>. </p>
<p>Now, by the <b>Extreme Value Theorem</b>, we can conclude that the function has <b>absolute extrema in the constraint region.</b></p>
<p>The next step is to write down the system of Lagrange Multiplier equations:</p>
<p>\( \qquad \qquad {@eqn1@}\) \(…\) equation \(1\)</p>
<p>\( \qquad \qquad {@eqn2@}\) \(…\) equation \(2\) </p>
<p>\( \qquad \qquad {@eqn3@}\) \(…\) equation \(3\) </p>
<p>\( \qquad \qquad {@constraint_eqn@}\) (Constraint equation) \(…\) equation \(4\) </p>
<p>Rewriting the second equation gives</p>
<p>\(\qquad \qquad {@solv_eqn1@}\) </p>
<p>\( \rightarrow  y=0\) (<b>case </b>\(1\)) or \(\lambda=1\)(<b>case </b>\(2\))</p>
<p>When \(y=0\)</p>
<p>\(\qquad \qquad {@eqn4@} \qquad  z=\pm {@ta7@}\)</p>
<p><b>case </b>\(1:\)  Plugging in the value of \(y\) into the constraint equation</p>
<p>Hence two potential absolute extrema points are
</p>
<p>\(\qquad \qquad {@ta8@}\) and \({@ta9@}\)</p>
<p><b>case </b>\(2:\) Plugging in the value of \(\lambda=1\) into equation \(3\)</p>
<p>\(\qquad  {@ev(eqn3,lambda=1)@}  \qquad z={@ta10@}\)</p>
<p>Plugging in the value of \(x=0\) and \(z={@ta10@}\) in \({@gx@}\) </p>
<p>\(\qquad \qquad   {@subst(rhs(z_2),z,subst(0,x,constraint_eqn))@} \qquad y=\pm {@ta11@}\)</p>
<p>This part gives us two more points that are potential absolute extrema,</p>
<p>\(\qquad \qquad {@ta12@}\) and \({@ta13@}\)</p>
<p>To find the absolute extrema we evaluate the function at each of these points</p>
<p>\(f\left({@ta12@}\right)={@globalmax@}, \) \(f\left({@ta13@}\right)={@globalmaxx@}, \)<p>\(f\left({@ta8@}\right)={@globalmin@}\) and \(f\left({@ta9@}\right)={@globalminn@}\)</p></p>
<p>The absolute maximum is then \({@ta14@}\) at \({@ta12@}\) and \({@ta13@}\)</p>
<p>The absolute minimum is \({@ta15@}\) at \({@ta8@}\) and \({@ta9@}\)</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
